  "interpretation": {
   "traffic_granularity": "18 B blocks are smaller than a 64 B cache line (288 blocks x 18 B = 5184 B = exactly 81 lines per row), so a scattered block mask keeps most lines alive. Measured traffic (touch kernel) sits between the two bounds: at 80% skip the modeled byte ratio is 0.20x, the 64 B-line ratio is 0.65x and the measured touch-time ratio is 0.66x (consistent with 32 B sector granularity); at 20% skip the touch ratio 0.88x matches the byte ratio 0.80x. The 18 B byte ratio is the optimistic bound, the 64 B-line ratio the pessimistic one.",
   "scatter_penalty": "A lone 18 B block still costs a full line fill and breaks sequential prefetch, so the same 58 kept blocks (2.67 MB addressed either way) give 3.39x when packed contiguously but only 1.71x when they are the scattered top-energy blocks.",
   "compute": "The dense kernel is compute-bound: 0.722 ms hot vs 0.546 ms pure-traffic touch for the same bytes, so skipping removes work as well as traffic. Hot (L3-resident) time scales with kept blocks (4.24x at 80% skip); cold/DRAM time scales with cache lines (1.71x).",
   "accuracy": "keep = top-energy 32-blocks of the synthetic activation; rel_l2 0.064 at 20% skip (0.996 of block energy kept) up to 0.423 at 80% skip (0.811 kept), so the 80% row is a traffic/compute bound, not a usable quality point."
